# .gitleaksignore — accepted findings, one fingerprint per line.
# AI Security Roadmap control 4.1.
#
# A fingerprint identifies ONE finding: <commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>
# (copy it verbatim from the pipeline log or the gitleaks-report.json artifact).
# Use this file only for:
#   * a historical secret that has been ROTATED/REVOKED and cannot be removed
#     from history without a rewrite the team has decided not to do;
#   * a confirmed false positive that is too specific for a .gitleaks.toml
#     allowlist.
# Every fingerprint is preceded by a comment LINE: what it was, why it is safe
# now, who accepted it and when. gitleaks does NOT accept trailing comments on
# the fingerprint line itself (verified with 8.30.1 — the entry is silently
# ignored). Never add a fingerprint for a secret that is still valid.
#
# Example:
# dev Mailgun key, revoked 2026-09-22 (Jose); value now injected by CI variable
# 3f2a9c1e0b7d4a5f6c8e9d0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c:src/config/dev.env:generic-api-key:12

# ── Accepted findings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Twenty-one jwt hits, all in upstream LiveKit commits (livekit/rust-sdks, authored
# by LiveKit developers 2022-2025; every commit is an ancestor of upstream/main),
# found by gitleaks 8.30.1 over the full history of origin/main (740 commits) on
# 2026-11-05 with the same config the SecretScan stage uses (this repo's
# .gitleaks.toml + the DisplayNote dn-credential-call-argument rule). Each is a
# LiveKit access token signed with one of LiveKit's own API keys for LiveKit's own
# example/test servers, or a unit-test fixture. None was issued by or for
# DisplayNote, none grants access to DisplayNote infrastructure, and only LiveKit
# can revoke them. Issuer and expiry come from the decoded claims (the signature
# was not used). Accepted by Lucio Aragon 2026-11-05.
#
# Exception to "never add a fingerprint for a secret that is still valid": that
# rule is for credentials DisplayNote owns and can rotate. Eight of these tokens
# carry an expiry in 2030 or 2045, and we cannot tell whether LiveKit's issuing keys
# still exist. They are third-party credentials that their owner published in its
# own public repository (livekit/rust-sdks) in 2022-2023, so rewriting this fork's
# history would not reduce their exposure, and rotating them is LiveKit's call.
# Any new finding in a DisplayNote commit gets the normal rule: rotate first.
